                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-39",
                                    "linecount": 61,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1197.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nrev_sdn_room — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA physical shoebox room built as a scattering delay network. One node sits on\neach of the six walls, exactly where the first reflection from the source to\nthe listener hits it; the nodes pass sound to each other along delay lines as\nlong as the real distances, and each wall absorbs (absorb) and darkens\n(wall_hz). So the early reflections land at their true times and the decay\nfollows the room's Sabine time. Two spaced omni receivers give the stereo.\n\nSource: Fontana et al., DAFx-24 Paper 34 §2.2–2.3 Eq. 2, 3, 6 and §3.2\n(SDN: wall nodes, lossless scattering matrix A = 2/K·11ᵀ − I times\nsqrt(1 − alpha), source/receiver lines and gains, wall filters); Mezza et al.,\nDAFx-25 Paper 51 §2.2 (scattering junction).\n\nThe point of this one is a real, small space around the tuba (concrete\nbasement, booth, garage) that adds punch and size with early reflections and\nno long wash, so it stays out of the way of the sub. 100% wet, no dry/wet.\nThe direct (line-of-sight) sound is left out.\n\nPARAMETERS (7 + output gain)\n\n1. predelay_ms — 0…100 ms, default 0 ms\n   Extra gap before the room (the geometry already gives ~10 ms at 6 m).\n\n2. room_m — 2…30 m, default 6 m\n   Room length; width is 0.8x and height 0.45x. Default T60 is ~0.35 s.\n   2–4 m = booth/closet slap, 6–10 m = studio/garage, 20–30 m = warehouse.\n   Moving it glides (the walls move, so the reflections Doppler-bend).\n\n3. absorb — 0.05…0.9, default 0.3\n   Wall absorption coefficient. 0.05 = bare concrete (long, ringing), 0.3 =\n   normal room, 0.7–0.9 = dead, early reflections only.\n\n4. wall_hz — 500…16000 Hz, default 5000 Hz\n   Lowpass at every wall bounce: soft furnishings low, hard tiles high.\n\n5. source_pos — 0.1…0.9, default 0.5\n   How far across the room the tuba stands from the receivers. Close = more\n   distinct early slaps; far = more diffuse and later.\n\n6. width — 0…1, default 0.8\n   Receiver spacing, 0 to min(2.5 m, 0.4 x room). 0 = mono.\n\n7. lowcut_hz — 20…600 Hz, default 120 Hz\n   Two one-pole highpasses on the source.\n\n8. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final output trim. Does not count toward the 8-param limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo audio (summed: one source in the room).\n- out1 / out2: left/right receivers, wet only.\n\nNOTES\n- 48 delay lines (30 node-to-node, 6 from the source, 12 to the receivers).\n- Level drops as the room grows (1/r spreading), ~5 dB from 2 m to 30 m.\n- Scattering is lossless before the wall loss, so it is stable at any setting.\n- No limiter, no dry path.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
