                                    },
                                    "text": "param out_gain_db 0 @min -24 @max 12",
                                    "varname": "out_gain_db"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-45",
                                    "linecount": 93,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1816.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nrev_spring_dispersion_tank_v2 — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA single-spring reverb tank. The signal is pushed through a delay loop whose\nround trip contains a 12-stage first-order allpass chain, so each echo is a\ndownward chirp instead of a click — the \"boing/drip\" of a Hammond-style spring.\nIn v2 the spring has mass: spring_ms is not read raw, it is the rest length of\na damped spring, so moving it stretches, overshoots, bounces and rattles.\nSource: Parker & Bilbao, \"Spring Reverberation: A Physical Perspective\",\nDAFx-09 Paper 84 §3 (echo spacing TD, dispersion transition frequency FC);\nGamper, Parker & Välimäki, \"Automated Calibration of a Parametric Spring Reverb\nModel\", DAFx-11 Paper 39 §2 (dispersion allpasses in a delay loop).\n\n100% wet, no dry/wet. Place it on an aux send; the dry tuba stays outside.\n\nPARAMETERS (8 + output gain)\n\n1. predelay_ms — 0…150 ms, default 10 ms\n   Gap before the spring is hit. 0–20 ms keeps the drip glued to the tongue;\n   80 ms+ pulls the tank behind the note. Slewed (~40 ms), so moving it glides\n   like a tape delay instead of clicking. Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n2. spring_ms — 15…90 ms, default 45 ms\n   Echo spacing, now exact (loop delay is compensated). Short = tight, bright,\n   \"sproing\"; long = looser, more cavernous. Moving it bends the pitch of every\n   echo (shorter = up, longer = down), because the echoes recirculate through\n   the moving delay.\n\n3. disperse — 0…1, default 0.6\n   Amount of allpass chirp. At 0 the tank is a true plain comb echo; at 1 the\n   echoes smear into a long downward glide. Input now enters before the chain,\n   so even the first echo chirps.\n\n4. decay_s — 0.4…8 s, default 2.5 s\n   RT60 of the tank (measured within ~3% over the whole range, every spring\n   length). Loop gain is derived from this and the live spring length, so long\n   springs decay in fewer round trips for the same setting. Level stays within\n   ~1 dB from 0.4 to 8 s. Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n5. damp_hz — 800…12000 Hz, default 3500 Hz\n   One-pole lowpass inside the loop. Low values darken each pass (vintage tank),\n   high values keep the metallic top. Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n6. width — 0…1, default 0.7\n   Mid/side spread of the two slightly detuned spring lines (the right line is\n   3.7% longer). 0 = mono tank, 1 = wide. Because a spring is a comb, a\n   sustained tuba note can sit on a comb peak in one line and between peaks\n   in the other, so the two sides can differ by a couple of dB on a held note\n   (most at long springs).\n\n7. elastic — 0…1, default 0.6\n   How heavy and springy the spring body is when spring_ms moves.\n     0     = instant jump (click/clang, no pitch glide)\n     ~0.3  = fast critically-damped glide: a quick pitched \"zip\"\n     ~0.6  = glide with one visible overshoot: \"sproing\"\n     1.0   = slow (about 2.4 Hz) and underdamped: wobbles several times, dubby\n             dive-and-recover\n   Glide speed is capped so the echoes never play backwards (pitch ratio stays\n   between about 0.2x and 1.8x).\n\n8. whack — 0…1, default 0.35\n   Clang and rattle injected into the loop when spring_ms moves: a burst on\n   each jump of the knob (~30 ms decay) plus a continuous rattle proportional\n   to the spring's speed. Passes through the dispersion chain, so it chirps.\n   Works with silent input (bumping the tank). 0 = silent movement.\n\n9. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final output trim, after all processing. Does not count toward the 8-param\n   limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo audio.\n- in3: connected but unused (kept so the batch inlet layout is identical).\n- in4: one-bar phasor, connected but unused — a spring has no tempo-locked time.\n- out1 / out2: stereo wet only.\n\nNOTES\n- Level is energy-normalised against the loop gain, so changing decay_s does\n  not change loudness much.\n- Loop gain is capped at 0.995 (a guard only: the allpasses, lowpass and\n  interpolation are all ≤ 1 and the 20 Hz DC blocker peaks at 1.0013); the\n  tank cannot run away — 60 s of 15↔90 ms knob slams at full elastic, whack,\n  disperse and decay end below −260 dB. The loop also has a gentle tanh\n  (unity at small signal, soft ceiling around 1.4), which only colours hot\n  or whacked signal.\n- Tension coupling: while the spring stretches the chirp steepens, while it\n  compresses the chirp relaxes. Only active when elastic > 0.\n- Delay buffers are 32768 samples, enough for the full range up to 192 kHz.\n- No pitch tracking, no bar sync, no limiter.\n- The whack burst uses two declared noise generators (@state ... = new noise(),\n  read with .next()), one per channel.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
