                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-41",
                                    "linecount": 95,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1854.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nrev_plate_dattorro — Parameter Information   (revision 3)\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA plate reverb built on the Dattorro (JAES 1997) topology as implemented in\nBeltrán et al., \"Matlab Implementation of Reverberation Algorithms\", DAFx-99 §3\nand Table 1: predelay → bandwidth lowpass → four input allpasses → two\ncross-coupled tank halves. Each half (sample counts at 29.76 kHz, scaled to\nyour sample rate) is a modulated decay-diffusion allpass (672 / 908, −0.70) →\ndelay (4453 / 4217) → damping lowpass → decay gain → decay-diffusion allpass\n(1800 / 2656, 0.5) → delay (3720 / 3163) → decay gain, then on into the other\nhalf. The stereo output is the seven taps per side of Table 1, read from\ninside those delays and allpasses.\n\nDense, bright and fast to build up — the classic plate for snare and vocal\nthrows. The tail is at full density within ~20 ms and decays smoothly (the\nrevision 2 tank, with four end-of-line taps, had a ~12 dB hole at ~130 ms and\na second swell after it). 100% wet, no dry/wet: put it on an aux send or\nreplace the dry outside.\n\nPARAMETERS (7 + output gain)\n\n1. predelay_ms — 0…200 ms, default 20 ms\n   Gap before the plate. 0–15 ms welds the plate to the transient; 40–200 ms\n   pushes it behind the note for a bigger perceived room. Changes glide rather\n   than jump, so moving it live is click-free (it takes a moment to arrive, and\n   large moves give a brief pitch glide on the input). Slider tapered\n   (exponent 2).\n\n2. decay_s — 0.8…20 s, default 5.0 s\n   RT60 of the tank. The decay gain (applied twice per half, as in the paper)\n   is solved from the full half-loop length (both allpasses, both delays and\n   the cross-feedback sample, averaged over the two halves), so the dialled\n   time is true at any sample rate (measured 1.0 / 2.0 / 5.0 / 19.2 s for\n   0.8 / 2 / 5 / 20 s with damping open). Damping makes the highs die sooner\n   than the dialled figure. Level rises ~4 dB from 0.8 to 20 s on the tuba.\n   Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n3. damp_hz — 500…16000 Hz, default 6000 Hz\n   One-pole lowpass in each tank half. 2–4 kHz makes a dark, old plate;\n   10 kHz+ keeps the metal bright. Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n4. diffuse — 0…1, default 0.65\n   Gain of the four input allpasses (0.40…0.75). Low = discrete early echoes\n   before the tail; high = the tail starts immediately with no flutter.\n\n5. wobble_ms — 0…3 ms, default 0.8 ms\n   Depth of the slow modulation on the tank's first (−0.70) decay-diffusion\n   allpasses, as in the paper. This is what stops a plate from ringing\n   metallically on sustained notes. The right half is modulated a quarter\n   cycle behind the left. (Dattorro's own excursion is ~0.5 ms; 3 ms is a\n   deliberate chorus.)\n\n6. wobble_hz — 0.05…2 Hz, default 0.35 Hz\n   Rate of that modulation. Below 0.2 Hz it reads as drift, above 1 Hz as a\n   subtle chorus on the tail. Slider tapered (exponent 2).\n\n7. width — 0…1, default 1.0\n   Mid/side spread of the two output tap sets. 0 collapses to mono, 1 is the\n   full image: on an impulse the two sides are uncorrelated (r ≈ 0.00), on a\n   mono tuba r ≈ 0.14, balanced within ~1 dB.\n\n8. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final output trim, after everything. Does not count toward the 8-param limit.\n\nAll parameters except predelay_ms are smoothed over roughly 20 ms, so automation\nand fast sweeps do not zipper.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo audio.\n- in3: connected but unused (keeps the batch inlet layout identical).\n- in4: one-bar phasor, connected but unused — a plate has no tempo-locked time.\n- out1 / out2: stereo wet only.\n\nNOTES\n- Decay diffusion allpass gains are fixed at −0.70 (modulated) and 0.5, as in\n  Dattorro's tank. Every element of the tank has gain ≤ 1 and the decay gain\n  is capped at 0.97 (reached only past 20 s), so it cannot run away — a 90 s\n  tail at the extremes ends below −280 dB.\n- Level is normalised against the half-pass gain; the default sits ~4 dB\n  under the dry tuba.\n- The bandwidth lowpass uses Dattorro's fixed coefficient of 0.9995, which is\n  effectively transparent; it is there for fidelity to the paper, not as a tone\n  control.\n- All delay lengths and output tap positions are rounded to whole samples\n  after scaling from the paper's 29.76 kHz table. Only the modulated\n  decay-diffusion allpasses are fractional (for the wobble).\n- Buffers are sized so the plate keeps its full length up to about 218 kHz; the\n  200 ms predelay range holds at any rate up to about 327 kHz.\n- A sub-audible alternating offset (about -360 dB) on the input keeps long\n  silent tails from running into denormal numbers.\n- The input stays stereo: the left input feeds the left half and the right\n  input the right half (Dattorro sums to mono first); on a mono tuba that is\n  the same thing.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
